# AM BUSINESS OS — MASTER EXECUTION TO FINAL HANDOFF

## Purpose

This is the **single authoritative execution issue** for completing AM Business OS from the current repository state through final customer handoff.

It supersedes Issue #3 and all previous phase/closure execution issues.

This is NOT a documentation exercise.
This is NOT a cosmetic Odoo clone.
This is NOT a request to rebuild the ERP from scratch.

The mission is:

**Audit the actual repository → strengthen/re-architect the core where required → implement missing capabilities → integrate modules → test real runtime behavior → prove accounting/inventory truth → harden security/reliability → standardize UX → prepare deployment → final customer acceptance → release/handoff.**

Do not ask the owner to restate requirements or confirm each phase.
Do not stop after describing work.
Execute continuously until a real blocker requires an explicit external dependency.
Record the blocker and continue with everything that can be completed without it.

---

# 0. AUTHORITATIVE BASELINE

Repository:

`amconsultantplatform-ctrl/AM-Business-Platform`

Branch:

`main`

Audited HEAD:

`51237a9a702f2c017a43b7bc032cda32c9ff25d4`

Current disposition:

`PRODUCTION CLOSED: NO`

The repository already contains a substantial ERP footprint, including:

- React + TypeScript + Vite frontend;
- Node.js + Express + TypeScript backend;
- SQLite with WAL persistence;
- authentication/RBAC/tenant/company controls;
- fiscal periods and closing;
- canonical financial-event / GL engines;
- AR/AP;
- sales;
- purchasing/procurement;
- inventory;
- inventory costing;
- manufacturing/MRP;
- quality/shop-floor related engines;
- PLM/manufacturing variants;
- treasury/banking;
- fixed assets;
- HR/payroll;
- POS;
- CRM;
- projects;
- reporting/BI;
- compliance adapters;
- multiple industry vertical engines;
- onboarding/branding;
- browser and runtime certification suites.

The codebase also contains a very large legacy `server.ts`, a generic `pilot_entities` persistence layer, many historical hardening suites, and several certification documents whose claims must be revalidated against the implementation.

Do NOT assume that a capability is complete because a file, route, engine, screen, or certification document exists.

---

# 1. NON-NEGOTIABLE ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLE

AM must remain its own product.

Current approved technology direction:

`React + TypeScript + Vite`
→ `Express + TypeScript`
→ SQLite for the current on-prem/local deployment architecture.

Odoo 19 is the **primary behavioral, functional, data-model and operating-model benchmark**.

Do NOT rewrite AM into Python simply to resemble Odoo.

Do NOT copy Odoo branding, proprietary assets, exact UI pixels, or Enterprise-only code.

Study Odoo Community 19 source/documentation where useful for behavior and architecture. Before directly reusing any Odoo source code, perform the required license/compliance review and document the decision.

Target:

**AM architecture remains AM; AM capability depth, accounting integrity, inventory truth, workflow maturity, UX consistency, configurability and extensibility reach Odoo-level breadth/depth.**

---

# 2. GLOBAL QUALITY STANDARD

Use Odoo 19 as the primary ERP benchmark plus applicable global accounting/security standards.

Minimum control references include:

- Odoo 19 functional and developer documentation;
- IFRS / IAS requirements applicable to the relevant accounting flows;
- IAS 2 for inventory measurement/cost formulas and lower-of-cost-and-NRV principles;
- IFRS 15 where revenue recognition is applicable;
- IAS 21 for foreign currency;
- IAS 16 / IAS 36 for fixed assets and impairment where applicable;
- OWASP ASVS 5.0 for application/API/security controls;
- least privilege, segregation of duties, immutable auditability, backup/restore and disaster-recovery principles.

The implementation must be adapted to the client's jurisdiction and business model. Do not claim legal/statutory certification without the relevant external authority evidence.

---

# 3. DEFINITION OF DONE

A capability is NOT complete because:

- a button exists;
- a route exists;
- an engine exists;
- a TypeScript test passes;
- documentation says PASS;
- a demo fixture passes.

A capability is complete only when the relevant scope has:

- correct business workflow;
- durable normalized/authoritative data;
- authenticated authorization;
- tenant/company/branch/warehouse scope;
- workflow/state control;
- period/date control;
- idempotency/duplicate protection;
- audit/provenance;
- accounting impact where applicable;
- inventory impact where applicable;
- source-document traceability;
- restart survival;
- HTTP/runtime proof;
- negative-path proof;
- reporting/drill-down;
- browser acceptance where relevant;
- performance/operational behavior appropriate to its deployment;
- no fake success/dead affordances.

---

# 4. PHASE 1 — CORE ARCHITECTURE AND DATA MODEL

Before expanding features, perform a complete architectural audit and establish a stable core.

## 4.1 Core domain model

Define canonical entities and relationships for at least:

- Company;
- Branch;
- Warehouse;
- Location/Bin;
- User;
- Role;
- Permission;
- Customer;
- Supplier;
- Product;
- Product Variant;
- UOM;
- UOM Category;
- Product Category;
- Tax;
- Currency;
- Exchange Rate;
- Fiscal Year;
- Fiscal Period;
- Journal;
- Journal Entry;
- Journal Line;
- Account;
- Analytic Account/Dimension;
- Bank/Cash Account;
- Payment;
- Payment Allocation;
- AR/AP Open Item;
- Inventory Move;
- Inventory Valuation Layer;
- Lot;
- Serial;
- Reservation;
- Inventory Adjustment;
- Purchase Request;
- RFQ;
- Purchase Order;
- Receipt/GRN;
- Vendor Bill;
- Sales Quotation;
- Sales Order;
- Delivery;
- Customer Invoice;
- Customer Payment;
- Credit/Debit Note;
- Manufacturing Order;
- BOM;
- BOM Version;
- Routing;
- Work Center;
- Work Order;
- WIP;
- Production Receipt;
- Scrap;
- Quality Inspection;
- Maintenance Order;
- Asset;
- Depreciation;
- Employee;
- Contract;
- Payroll Run;
- Expense;
- Project;
- Task;
- Timesheet;
- CRM Lead/Opportunity;
- POS Session;
- POS Order;
- Document/Attachment;
- Activity;
- Approval;
- Audit Event;
- Notification;
- Background Job.

The exact table/model implementation is subject to architectural audit, but the relationships must be explicit and durable.

## 4.2 Persistence architecture

The current generic `pilot_entities` model may remain for suitable configuration/non-critical records, but critical financial and inventory data must have a strong authoritative persistence contract.

Audit whether critical domains require normalized tables/repositories with:

- foreign keys;
- unique business keys;
- immutable posted records;
- optimistic/version checks where needed;
- transactional writes;
- precise queries;
- constraints;
- deterministic numbering;
- durable history.

Do not allow generic JSON storage to become an excuse for weak accounting/inventory integrity.

Migration from the current persistence layer must be incremental and backward-safe.

## 4.3 Modular code organization

The current monolithic `server.ts` must be gradually decomposed without a big-bang rewrite.

Target concept:

`modules/<domain>/`

with:

- domain types/models;
- application services;
- repositories/persistence;
- controllers/routes;
- authorization policies;
- workflow definitions;
- reports;
- tests.

Keep central/shared services for:

- identity;
- scope;
- transaction management;
- numbering;
- audit;
- search;
- notifications;
- configuration;
- canonical financial posting;
- inventory valuation interfaces.

Remove duplicated domain logic only after all dependencies and tests are migrated.

---

# 5. PHASE 2 — ACCOUNTING KERNEL

This is the highest-priority core.

Do not proceed to final system certification until the accounting kernel is demonstrably trustworthy.

## 5.1 General Ledger

Implement/verify:

- Chart of Accounts hierarchy;
- account types and normal balances;
- account activation/deactivation;
- parent/child relationships;
- posting restrictions;
- journals;
- journal entries;
- multi-line journal support;
- balanced double entry;
- journal numbering;
- source-document reference;
- created/approved/posted/reversed states;
- immutable posted entries;
- reversal/adjustment mechanism;
- attachments;
- audit trail;
- dimensions;
- branch/company scope;
- fiscal year/period;
- closed-period protection.

## 5.2 Subledger architecture

AR, AP, Inventory, Assets, Payroll, Treasury and other financial domains must produce authoritative financial events/journal entries through the same accounting core.

No module may maintain an independent GL.

Every posting must support:

**source document → subledger effect → financial event → journal entry → journal lines → audit**

## 5.3 AR

Complete and prove:

- customer master;
- credit limits;
- payment terms;
- invoices;
- credit notes;
- customer receipts;
- allocations;
- partial settlements;
- write-offs;
- overdue/aging;
- statements;
- promises/collections;
- reversals;
- multi-currency;
- realized FX;
- revenue recognition where required;
- reconciliation to GL.

## 5.4 AP

Complete and prove:

- supplier master;
- purchase invoice;
- duplicate invoice protection;
- 2/3-way matching as applicable;
- vendor credits/debits;
- payment proposals;
- payment batches;
- allocations;
- early payment discounts;
- supplier aging/statements;
- accruals;
- FX;
- reversals;
- reconciliation to GL.

## 5.5 Taxes

Build a proper tax abstraction:

- tax definitions;
- tax groups;
- inclusive/exclusive taxes;
- line-level taxes;
- tax exemptions;
- tax mapping/fiscal positions;
- tax accounting;
- tax return calculation;
- tax carry-forward;
- jurisdiction adapters;
- validation and rounding.

For Egypt/customer jurisdiction, implement the relevant official localization as an adapter rather than hard-coding one country into the kernel.

## 5.6 Bank/Treasury

Prove:

- bank accounts;
- cash accounts;
- bank statements/transactions;
- payments;
- matching;
- reconciliation models;
- fees;
- transfers;
- cash controls;
- outstanding items;
- bank reconciliation;
- FX differences;
- audit.

## 5.7 Analytic Accounting

Provide:

- analytic accounts;
- dimensions;
- analytic plans;
- cost centers;
- profit centers;
- projects;
- allocation rules;
- split allocations;
- profitability reporting.

Analytic dimensions must remain linked to source journal lines and reporting.

## 5.8 Fixed Assets

Complete:

- asset registration;
- capitalization;
- acquisition;
- useful life;
- depreciation methods;
- depreciation schedules;
- disposals;
- transfers;
- impairment;
- revaluation where applicable;
- maintenance interaction;
- audit;
- GL integration;
- period/close control.

## 5.9 Closing and financial integrity

Prove:

- month-end close;
- year-end close;
- closing journal;
- retained earnings;
- next fiscal year;
- opening balances;
- locked periods;
- reopening with controlled authorization;
- audit trail;
- restart persistence.

---

# 6. PHASE 3 — INVENTORY / COSTING KERNEL

This is the second highest-priority core and must be treated as a financial subledger, not only a stock counter.

## 6.1 Canonical inventory ledger

Build/verify an immutable movement model containing:

- source document;
- movement type;
- date/time;
- company;
- branch;
- warehouse;
- location;
- product;
- variant;
- UOM;
- quantity;
- direction;
- lot/serial;
- unit cost;
- total value;
- user;
- correlation/idempotency key.

## 6.2 Stock availability

Implement/prove:

- on-hand;
- available;
- reserved;
- incoming;
- outgoing;
- committed;
- in-transit;
- damaged;
- quarantined;
- returned;
- negative-stock policy;
- reservations;
- releases;
- backorders;
- partial receipts/deliveries.

## 6.3 Costing

Support and prove:

- FIFO;
- AVCO/moving weighted average;
- Standard Cost;
- specific identification where required by inventory nature;
- cost layers;
- layer consumption;
- returns;
- reversals;
- landed costs;
- currency;
- rounding;
- cost adjustments;
- audit of every valuation change.

Cost calculations must be deterministic and replayable from persisted source movements.

## 6.4 Inventory accounting

For relevant transactions, prove:

**Receipt → valuation → inventory asset effect → AP/accrual effect**

**Sale/Delivery → cost-layer consumption → COGS → inventory reduction**

**Return → reversal/reinstatement of correct cost**

**Adjustment → controlled variance → GL**

**Manufacturing consumption → WIP → production output → finished-goods valuation**

The report must reconcile inventory subledger valuation to the canonical GL.

## 6.5 IAS 2 controls

Where applicable:

- inventory cost contains purchase/conversion/other appropriate costs;
- interchangeable inventory uses a consistent permitted cost formula;
- inventory carrying value can be assessed against NRV;
- write-downs and reversals are traceable;
- production WIP includes appropriate conversion costs;
- no arbitrary zero/default cost fallback.

## 6.6 Physical inventory

Complete:

- count sessions;
- blind counting;
- cycle counting;
- approvals;
- variances;
- recount;
- adjustment proposals;
- posting;
- period locks;
- audit;
- snapshot;
- as-of reporting.

## 6.7 Warehouse operations

Complete:

- multiple warehouses;
- locations/bins;
- put-away;
- picking;
- packing;
- delivery;
- internal transfer;
- transit;
- receipts;
- returns;
- lot/serial;
- barcode;
- packages where applicable.

---

# 7. PHASE 4 — SOURCE-TO-CASH / SOURCE-TO-PAY

Every major transaction must be traceable end-to-end.

## Sales

Quotation → Sales Order → Delivery → Invoice → Payment → Reconciliation

Include:

- pricing;
- pricelists;
- discounts;
- taxes;
- credit limits;
- approval;
- partial delivery;
- backorders;
- returns;
- credit notes;
- subscriptions where applicable.

## Purchases

Requisition → RFQ → Vendor Quotation → Purchase Order → Receipt → Vendor Bill → Payment → Reconciliation

Include:

- approvals;
- budgets;
- vendor pricing;
- partial receipt;
- three-way match;
- returns;
- accruals.

## Commercial/CRM

Lead → Opportunity → Activity → Quotation → Customer → Sale

Activities must be real, persistent and permission-aware.

---

# 8. PHASE 5 — MANUFACTURING / MRP

Use Odoo 19 MRP behavior as the benchmark for breadth and workflow.

Complete/prove:

- BOM;
- BOM versions;
- variants;
- routing;
- work centers;
- capacity;
- operations;
- work orders;
- material reservations;
- consumption;
- scrap;
- substitutions where supported;
- WIP;
- finished goods;
- production receipts;
- actual cost;
- labor;
- overhead;
- variance;
- byproducts/co-products where required;
- subcontracting;
- shop-floor execution;
- quality;
- maintenance;
- PLM/ECO;
- manufacturing traceability;
- full GL linkage.

The current manufacturing transaction coordinator blocker must be fixed first:

- no stale snapshot overwrite;
- no lost update;
- durable idempotency;
- real HTTP concurrency proof;
- real rollback proof;
- restart proof;
- correct effective posting date;
- durable audit.

Finished-goods receipt and work-order settlement must have explicit complete scope before MRP is certified.

Official Odoo 19 MRP scope includes basic setup, workflows, advanced configuration, shop floor, reporting, subcontracting, quality control and maintenance.

---

# 9. PHASE 6 — QUALITY / MAINTENANCE / PLM / REPAIRS

Existing engines must be audited against full workflows rather than counted as complete because files exist.

## Quality

- control points;
- inspections;
- pass/fail;
- quality alerts;
- quarantine;
- nonconformity;
- corrective action;
- traceability;
- source order linkage.

## Maintenance

- equipment;
- preventive maintenance;
- corrective maintenance;
- work orders;
- schedules;
- downtime;
- costs;
- manufacturing/work-center linkage.

## PLM

- engineering changes;
- approvals;
- versioning;
- ECO lifecycle;
- BOM impact;
- effective dates;
- change history.

## Repairs

- repair orders;
- diagnosis;
- parts;
- labor;
- stock effect;
- billing;
- accounting;
- lifecycle.

---

# 10. PHASE 7 — HR / PAYROLL / PEOPLE OPERATIONS

Complete the internal HR operating model to the depth actually required.

Benchmark Odoo 19 HR application breadth.

Include:

- employee master;
- departments;
- jobs;
- contracts;
- skills;
- certifications;
- equipment;
- onboarding;
- offboarding;
- attendance;
- leave/time off;
- recruitment;
- appraisals;
- expenses;
- planning;
- fleet where applicable;
- payroll;
- reporting.

Payroll must prove:

DRAFT → APPROVAL → POST → PAY

with:

- maker/checker;
- creator self-approval rejection;
- period control;
- GL posting;
- reconciliation;
- restart;
- duplicate protection;
- audit.

---

# 11. PHASE 8 — PROJECTS / SERVICES

Benchmark Odoo 19 Services.

Complete as applicable:

- projects;
- tasks;
- milestones;
- dependencies;
- planning;
- timesheets;
- field service;
- helpdesk;
- service contracts;
- recurring work;
- profitability;
- service billing.

Timesheets must be linked to employees/users with correct access controls.

---

# 12. PHASE 9 — POS / RETAIL / VERTICAL OPERATIONS

POS must become a real operational system, not a visual screen.

Complete/prove:

- POS configuration;
- sessions;
- opening;
- sales;
- payments;
- cash management;
- refunds;
- returns;
- customer linkage;
- taxes;
- discounts;
- cashier permissions;
- closing;
- session reconciliation;
- hardware;
- barcode;
- stock impact;
- accounting.

Where needed:

- restaurant floors/tables;
- kitchen/preparation;
- split bills;
- tips;
- self-order;
- loyalty;
- customer display.

Preserve current vertical engines for:

- commercial distribution;
- restaurant/F&B;
- mobile/electronics;
- fashion;
- apparel manufacturing;

but promote only those capabilities that pass full workflow/persistence/accounting tests to certified status.

---

# 13. PHASE 10 — WEBSITE / ECOMMERCE / MARKETING

Build only where required by the product scope, but target Odoo-level completeness for enabled capabilities.

## Website/eCommerce

- catalog;
- products/variants;
- pricing;
- taxes;
- customer accounts;
- cart;
- checkout;
- payments;
- shipping;
- delivery;
- order creation;
- returns;
- portal;
- SEO/configuration basics.

## Marketing

- email campaigns;
- SMS;
- marketing automation;
- social;
- events;
- surveys;
- segmentation;
- activities;
- campaign analytics.

External provider integrations remain explicit adapter boundaries.

---

# 14. PHASE 11 — DOCUMENTS / PRODUCTIVITY / PLATFORM

Complete the operating-system layer.

Include as applicable:

- Documents;
- attachments;
- Sign/e-signature workflow;
- Approvals;
- Activities;
- Chatter/history;
- notifications;
- calendar;
- discussion/comments;
- knowledge/content;
- dashboards;
- spreadsheet/reporting integration;
- scheduled actions;
- automation rules;
- imports/exports;
- configurable fields/properties;
- API integration;
- background jobs.

Every document/action must remain traceable to the source record.

---

# 15. PHASE 12 — ODOO-LEVEL UX / OPERATING MODEL

Only after core correctness is stable, standardize the UI as one operating system.

Create shared AM primitives for:

- List;
- Form;
- Kanban;
- Search;
- Filter;
- Group By;
- Sort;
- Pivot;
- Graph;
- Calendar where relevant;
- Gantt/timeline where justified;
- Dashboard;
- Record page;
- Smart links;
- Activities;
- History/audit;
- Attachments;
- workflow state bar;
- approvals;
- action menus.

Record pages must expose related business context.

Examples:

Sales Order → deliveries → invoices → payments → journal

Purchase Order → receipts → vendor bill → payment

Product → stock → valuation → purchases → sales → BOMs

Manufacturing Order → BOM → work orders → material issues → production → costs → journal

Customer/Supplier → invoices → payments → aging → statements

No generic object-dump production UX.

No visible action without a real backend workflow.

Use AM branding and design system.

Arabic RTL and English LTR must be first-class layouts, not translated-afterwards screens.

---

# 16. PHASE 13 — SEARCH / REPORTING / ANALYTICS

Reporting must be downstream of authoritative data.

Core accounting reports:

- Trial Balance;
- General Ledger;
- Balance Sheet;
- Profit & Loss;
- Cash Flow;
- AR Aging;
- AP Aging;
- customer/vendor statements;
- Tax reports;
- Audit Trail;
- journal reports;
- analytic reports;
- budgets;
- variance;
- fixed-asset/depreciation;
- inventory valuation;
- inventory movement;
- COGS;
- stock aging;
- profitability;
- branch/company comparison.

Every report must support appropriate:

- period;
- as-of date;
- scope;
- branch;
- warehouse;
- account/product/customer/supplier dimensions;
- search/filter/group;
- drill-down;
- export.

Key reports must reconcile to their canonical sources.

A dashboard KPI is accepted only when it traces to persisted source data.

No report may silently convert invalid/missing data to zero.

---

# 17. PHASE 14 — RECONCILIATION / CONTROL FRAMEWORK

Build one unified reconciliation framework for:

- AR;
- AP;
- Inventory;
- Assets;
- Bank;
- Tax;
- Payroll;
- Opening Balances;
- Manufacturing/WIP where applicable.

Each reconciliation must expose:

- scope;
- period/as-of;
- opening;
- movements;
- adjustments;
- closing;
- subledger balance;
- GL balance;
- difference;
- status;
- evidence/source;
- generated timestamp;
- audit/provenance.

Allowed states include:

- COMPLETED;
- EXCEPTION;
- PENDING;
- NOT_CONFIGURED;
- NOT_AVAILABLE;
- EXTERNAL.

Do not manufacture zeros to hide missing evidence.

---

# 18. PHASE 15 — SECURITY / GOVERNANCE / AUDIT

Target at least OWASP ASVS 5.0 Level 2 controls, and stronger controls for high-risk operations where justified.

Verify:

- secure authentication;
- password hashing;
- session lifecycle;
- token protection;
- optional MFA architecture for privileged users;
- authorization;
- object/record access;
- field sensitivity;
- anti-IDOR;
- tenant/company isolation;
- branch/warehouse scope;
- maker/checker;
- approval thresholds;
- privilege escalation resistance;
- secure file handling;
- secure configuration;
- API security;
- rate limiting where required;
- audit logging;
- error handling;
- secrets management.

Security failures must fail closed.

Audit events must capture sufficient metadata to reconstruct who/what/when/where without logging sensitive secrets.

---

# 19. PHASE 16 — RELIABILITY / CONCURRENCY / PERFORMANCE

Prove:

- transaction boundaries;
- concurrent business operations;
- idempotency;
- lost-update prevention;
- restart survival;
- process crash recovery;
- backup/restore;
- WAL handling;
- schema initialization;
- deterministic numbering;
- background job recovery;
- database integrity checks;
- predictable error handling.

Add load/concurrency tests for high-risk flows.

The system must not rely on one process's in-memory arrays as the authoritative source of truth.

---

# 20. PHASE 17 — DEPLOYMENT / HOSTING / MULTI-BRANCH

Define and implement the supported deployment model.

For the first customer:

- one central AM instance;
- one authoritative database;
- multiple branches;
- branch/warehouse scope;
- browser access;
- mobile access;
- HTTPS;
- persistent storage;
- automated backups;
- restore procedure;
- monitoring;
- release/versioning;
- rollback procedure.

Do NOT put shared SQLite WAL files on a network share.

Design the architecture so a future migration to PostgreSQL/server-class database can happen without rewriting the business domains.

Document three future deployment options:

1. central on-premise server;
2. hosted VPS/cloud server;
3. future SaaS/multi-tenant platform.

Offline POS/edge synchronization is a separate advanced capability and must not be faked. If enabled, it requires a real conflict-resolution/sync architecture.

---

# 21. PHASE 18 — DATA MIGRATION / IMPORT / EXPORT

Provide controlled onboarding for:

- company;
- branches;
- warehouses;
- chart of accounts;
- customers;
- suppliers;
- products;
- opening stock;
- opening balances;
- price lists;
- taxes;
- users;
- employees;
- assets where required.

Imports must support:

- validation;
- preview;
- reject/repair;
- all-or-nothing commit;
- audit;
- repeatability;
- duplicate protection.

Exports must be real PDF/XLSX/CSV as applicable and must respect scope/permissions.

---

# 22. PHASE 19 — TESTING STRATEGY

Adopt a layered test model inspired by the depth of Odoo's unit + integration + browser testing.

Every critical domain needs:

- unit tests;
- domain/integration tests;
- HTTP API tests;
- browser/end-user workflow tests;
- persistence/restart tests;
- negative-path tests;
- authorization/scope tests;
- concurrency tests;
- accounting reconciliation tests.

Do not certify an engine from a unit test if the production route is untested.

Do not certify an HTTP route without persistence/restart proof when persistence matters.

Build a permanent regression suite.

Temporary investigation scripts may be removed after stronger permanent tests replace them.

---

# 23. PHASE 20 — CI / RELEASE / ENVIRONMENT CONTROL

Establish reliable CI evidence tied to exact commits.

CI must include at least:

- clean install;
- typecheck/lint;
- build;
- full regression;
- accounting tests;
- inventory tests;
- manufacturing tests;
- HTTP security tests;
- browser acceptance;
- backup/restore;
- dependency/security audit;
- production-style first-run;
- migration/import checks.

Exact-SHA evidence must be recorded.

External CI permission failures must be documented as external, not reported as PASS.

Create release artifacts with:

- version;
- commit SHA;
- changelog;
- migration notes;
- deployment steps;
- rollback steps;
- verification checklist.

---

# 24. PHASE 21 — CLEANUP / TECHNICAL DEBT

During refactoring:

KEEP:

- valid production code;
- core persistence;
- accounting kernel;
- inventory kernel;
- security;
- authorization;
- permanent tests;
- migration tools still required;
- authoritative documentation.

