# 33. SIX-VERTICAL FIRST COMMERCIAL RELEASE ROADMAP

## 33.1 Scope correction

The first commercial AM release is not a one-customer/one-vertical prototype. It must support the six business activities already agreed with the owner in the first application/release.

The exact six activity names must be copied from the owner's approved vertical list before vertical-specific acceptance tests are frozen. Do not invent or substitute verticals. Until that list is attached, build and certify the shared core and define vertical packs as configurable extensions on the same core.

The six activities must NOT become six separate codebases, databases, accounting engines or product forks.

Architecture: one AM Core; shared canonical accounting, inventory, identity, audit, workflow, reporting and integration foundations; six configurable vertical packs, each with its own workflows, data requirements, validations, document types, reports and acceptance tests; customer/company/branch configuration determines which capabilities are enabled.

## 33.2 Release gates and execution sequence

### R0 — Baseline and scope lock

- Inspect current main HEAD and working tree.
- Re-run typecheck, build and permanent regression tests at exact SHA.
- Create a capability matrix: CERTIFIED / PARTIAL / NOT_CONFIGURED / EXTERNAL / NOT_APPLICABLE.
- Record implementation and evidence; do not infer completion from UI presence or old reports.
- Attach the owner's six approved activity names and map each to required workflows, data, reports, integrations and acceptance scenarios.
- Select a representative pilot customer/data set for each activity, while keeping all six verticals in first-release scope.

Exit: six vertical scope sheets approved; shared-core versus vertical-specific scope is explicit.

### R1 — Close transaction integrity P0

- Fix and permanently test the manufacturing HTTP restart-journal failure.
- Verify journal creation, serialization, retrieval, source-document linkage and persistence across actual server restart.
- Confirm the test queries the canonical journal source and correct source-document type; distinguish persistence defects from API projection/filter defects.
- Re-run concurrency, idempotency, rollback, lost-update, restart and audit tests over real HTTP routes.
- Verify tenant/company/branch scope and unauthorized/negative paths.
- Preserve exact-SHA test evidence.

Exit: no known critical transaction/persistence blocker in any workflow enabled for first release.

### R2 — Shared ERP Core

Build/verify once for all six activities:

- Tenant/company/branch/warehouse identity and scope;
- users, roles, permissions and maker/checker for sensitive operations;
- product/service/customer/supplier masters and units of measure;
- document numbering, lifecycle/statuses, approvals, attachments and audit;
- canonical GL, journal posting, fiscal periods and posting dates;
- AR/AP, receipts/payments, allocations and reconciliation;
- treasury/cash/bank and payment methods;
- inventory ledger, reservations/movements, costing/valuation and stock adjustments;
- taxes and configurable tax rules for actual jurisdiction/customer scope;
- durable persistence, idempotency, rollback, restart and controlled reversals;
- import validation, preview, error handling and auditable commit.

Exit: each enabled shared workflow is proven end-to-end over UI/API and database, not only in unit tests.

### R3 — Six Vertical Packs

For each of the six owner-approved activities, implement a separate vertical scope sheet and pack on the shared core.

Each pack must specify and prove:
1. Activity-specific master data and configuration;
2. source documents and complete document lifecycle;
3. operational workflow and approval states;
4. inventory/manufacturing/service effects as applicable;
5. accounting and tax mapping;
6. role/branch permissions;
7. activity-specific reports and KPIs;
8. import/onboarding templates;
9. failure, cancellation, return and reversal scenarios;
10. browser/API, persistence, restart and reconciliation acceptance tests.

The first release must not mark a vertical as enabled until its mandatory scenarios pass. A vertical that is not yet certified remains explicitly disabled or pilot-only; it must not silently produce incorrect postings or reports.

Exit: six vertical acceptance packs completed against real or representative customer scenarios.

### R4 — Reporting and management information

Treat reporting as a first-release core capability, not a later cosmetic phase.

Required shared reports, scoped to applicable activities:
- Trial Balance;
- General Ledger and journal drill-down;
- Profit & Loss;
- Balance Sheet;
- Cash Flow / treasury movements;
- AR/AP aging and customer/supplier statements;
- inventory on-hand, movement, aging and valuation;
- sales and purchase analysis;
- gross margin and profitability;
- expense analysis;
- branch/company comparison;
- manufacturing/WIP/cost/variance reports where applicable;
- activity-specific operational reports and KPIs for all six verticals.

Every report must have documented source tables/ledgers, scope, period/as-of semantics, filters, branch/activity dimensions, export, access control and reconciliation tests.

No fabricated zeros, untraceable KPI values, or dashboard-only calculations. Reports must drill down to source documents and ledger entries wherever applicable.

Exit: each required financial report reconciles to its canonical source; each vertical has its agreed operational report pack and acceptance evidence.

### R5 — AI and forecasting foundation (build the place now; defer advanced intelligence)

Establish the architecture now, without making AI prediction a blocker for first customer unless a specific customer requires it.

Foundation required in first release:
- analytics/AI module boundary separate from transaction posting and canonical accounting;
- versioned, documented read-only data contracts/views for approved operational and financial data;
- stable entity IDs and links to source documents, company, branch, product, customer, activity and time;
- governed metrics/semantic definitions so revenue, margin, stock, turnover and other KPIs mean the same thing across dashboards and AI;
- data freshness timestamps, lineage, provenance and quality/completeness flags;
- tenant/company/branch permission enforcement for every analytics query;
- explicit consent/configuration and security boundary for any external AI provider;
- provider/model adapter interface, so vendors can change without rewriting ERP domains;
- prompt/model/configuration version logging and audit for any enabled AI-assisted action;
- human review/approval before AI output can create or post financial, inventory, payroll or other consequential transactions;
- feature flags and disabled-by-default AI/forecasting capabilities;
- test datasets and evaluation harness locations for future forecasting.

Defer to later phases unless needed by a contracted first-customer use case:
- demand and sales forecasting;
- replenishment recommendations;
- cash-flow forecasting;
- predictive maintenance;
- anomaly detection beyond deterministic rules;
- natural-language analytics and AI agents;
- automated decisions or transaction posting.

Exit: architecture, data contracts, access controls, integration adapters and roadmap are present and tested; no claim of predictive accuracy or AI readiness without model-specific evidence.

### R6 — Deployment, continuity and mobile

For the first six-activity release:
- production-like central instance and authoritative database;
- multi-company/branch scope and branch-specific access;
- HTTPS, secrets management, persistent storage, monitoring and versioned releases;
- scheduled encrypted backups, integrity checks and documented restore;
- restore drill on a replacement environment/device;
- rollback and incident procedures;
- responsive mobile management for branch performance, key financial/operational reports, inventory, receivables/payables and alerts;
- visible last-updated and sync status.

Architect for cloud SaaS, hosted VPS/private cloud, central on-premise and future local/edge profiles without separate product forks. Cloud multi-tenant remains the primary target architecture, but deployment readiness must be evidenced per profile.

Offline/edge synchronization must not be represented as available until local durable transactions, outbox, idempotent sync, conflict handling and recovery tests pass. The exact first-release offline scope must be stated explicitly.

Exit: documented and rehearsed deployment, backup, restore, rollback and mobile acceptance.

### R7 — Six-customer onboarding and controlled go-live

Prepare six customer onboarding plans, one per agreed activity/customer, covering:
- legal entity, companies and branches;
- users, roles and training;
- chart of accounts, tax setup and financial periods;
- customer/supplier/product/service masters;
- opening balances and inventory;
- activity-specific setup, documents and workflows;
- migration validation and sign-off;
- reports and reconciliation baseline;
- support contact, severity/response procedure and issue log;
- acceptance sign-off and go-live rollback plan.

Use staged pilots and controlled activation; do not switch all six to live operations on the same day without evidence and support capacity. The first release must nevertheless contain the certified scope required for all six agreed activities.

Exit: customer-specific UAT signed off, opening balances reconciled, users trained, backup/restore verified, support ready, and go-live authorization recorded.

## 33.3 First-release critical path

R0 Scope lock → R1 Transaction integrity → R2 Shared core → R3 Six vertical packs → R4 Reports → R5 AI/analytics foundation (parallel with R2-R4 where safe) → R6 Deployment/continuity/mobile → R7 Customer onboarding and controlled go-live.

R5 foundation can proceed in parallel with core work only where it does not alter transaction truth or delay critical accounting, inventory and customer workflows. Predictive AI models are explicitly not on the first-customer critical path by default.

## 33.4 Evidence and progress reporting

For every release gate, record exact commit SHA; implementation PR/commit; test command and environment; pass/fail output; known limitations; affected verticals/customers; accounting and inventory reconciliation evidence; security/scope evidence; and decision PASS / BLOCKED / PARTIAL.

Do not report a phase as complete based on code presence, successful build alone, or a descriptive checklist.



